      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to consider the constraint that all the math books must stay together and all the English books must stay together. This means we can treat the group of math books as one item and the group of English books as another item.\n\n1. **Treat the math books as a single unit:**\n   Since all three math books must stay together, we can think of them as one \"super book.\" We will first arrange this \"super book.\"\n\n2. **Treat the English books as another unit:**\n   Similarly, all five English books must remain together, so they will also be treated as one \"super book.\"\n\n3. **Arrange the two units:**\n   Now, we have two objects to arrange: the \"super book of math books\" and the \"super book of English books.\"\n\n   The number of ways to arrange two distinct objects is given by \\(2!\\) (2 factorial):\n   \\[\n   2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Arrange the books within each unit:**\n   Within the \"super book of math books,\" the three math books can be arranged among themselves in \\(3!\\) (3 factorial) ways. Similarly, within the \"super book of English books,\" the five English books can be arranged among themselves in \\(5!\\) (5 factorial) ways.\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the total number of arrangements:**\n   The total number of ways to arrange the 8 books (the two \"super books\" and the 8 individual books) is the product of the number of ways to arrange the units and the number of ways to arrange the books within each unit.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total arrangements} = 6 \\times 720 \\times 120\n   \\]\n\n6. **Simplify the calculation:**\n   First, calculate \\(6 \\times 720\\):\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 720 = 4320\n   \\]\n   Next, multiply the result by 120:\n   \\[\n   4320 \\times 120 = 518400\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf, keeping both groups together, is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{518400}\n\\]",
