      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Solution: To solve the problem, we need to consider the arrangement of the groups of books and the books within each group.\n\nSince the 3 math books must stay together and the 5 English books must stay together, we can treat each group as a single \"super book.\" This means that we have 2 \"super books\" in total.\n\n1. Arrangement of the \"super books\": We have 2 \"super books,\" and we can arrange them in 2! (2 factorial) ways. This is because there are 2 positions, and they can be swapped.\n\n2. In each \"super book,\" the remaining books can be arranged. There are 3 math books in one \"super book\" and 5 English books in the other \"super book.\" They can be arranged in their respective groups.\n\nFor the math books, we need to arrange all 3 math books, which can be done in 3! (3 factorial) ways. For the English books, we need to arrange all 5 English books, which can be done in 5! (5 factorial) ways.\n\nThus, we need to account for the arrangement of the groups as well as the arrangements within each group.\n\nThe total number of ways to arrange the books is given by the permutation of the groups, multiplied by the arrangement of books within each group:\n\n= Arrangements of the groups * Arrangements within each group\n= 2! * 3! * 5!\n= 2! * 3! * 5! (where \"!\" denotes factorial)\n\nContinuing to calculate:\n\n= 2 * 3 * 2 * 1 * 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1\n= 2 * 3 * 2 * 1 * (5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1)\n= 12 * 120\n= 1440\n\nSo, there are 1440 ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf, with all the math books staying together and all the English books also staying together.",
